Well, according to MS-DOS, the Brazilian keyboard layout was identical
to the USAnian one, except that the quote and tilde were dead keys, used
for creating accented letters. For example, to enter an é, you typed 'e
(and to enter a ', you typed '<space>), much as the US-international
(_there_'s a nice oxymoron for you) layout under MS-Windows. So in that
case, the standard US layout was an international keyboard: both USAnian
and Brazilian.

This was quite useful for those of us in countries (i.e., .nl) whose
language uses some accents, but whose official MS keyboard layout is
both highly deficient and quite unlike the real customary national
typewriter layout, too.

